{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"P13001","secondaryAccessions":["Q2M777"],"uniProtkbId":"BIOH_ECOLI","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"1990-01-01","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2026-11-04","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"1990-01-01","entryVersion":187,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":5.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Escherichia coli (strain K12)","taxonId":83333,"lineage":["Bacteria","Pseudomonadati","Pseudomonadota","Gammaproteobacteria","Enterobacterales","Enterobacteriaceae","Escherichia"]},"proteinExistence":"1: Evidence at protein level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"Pimeloyl-[acyl-carrier protein] methyl ester esterase"},"ecNumbers":[{"value":"3.1.1.85"}]},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"value":"Biotin synthesis protein BioH"}},{"fullName":{"value":"Carboxylesterase BioH"}}]},"genes":[{"geneName":{"value":"bioH"},"synonyms":[{"value":"bioB"}],"orderedLocusNames":[{"value":"b3412"},{"value":"JW3375"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"value":"The physiological role of BioH is to remove the methyl group introduced by BioC when the pimeloyl moiety is complete. It allows to synthesize pimeloyl-ACP via the fatty acid synthetic pathway through the hydrolysis of the ester bonds of pimeloyl-ACP esters. E.coli employs a methylation and demethylation strategy to allow elongation of a temporarily disguised malonate moiety to a pimelate moiety by the fatty acid synthetic enzymes. BioH shows a preference for short chain fatty acid esters (acyl chain length of up to 6 carbons) and short chain p-nitrophenyl esters. Also displays a weak thioesterase activity. Can form a complex with CoA, and may be involved in the condensation of CoA and pimelic acid into pimeloyl-CoA, a precursor in biotin biosynthesis"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"value":"Catalyzes the hydrolysis of the methyl ester bond of dimethylbutyryl-S-methyl mercaptopropionate (DMB-S-MMP) to yield dimethylbutyryl mercaptopropionic acid (DMBS-MPA) during the biocatalytic conversion of simvastin acid from monacolin J acid. Can also use acyl carriers such as dimethylbutyryl-S-ethyl mercaptopropionate (DMB-S-EMP) and dimethylbutyryl-S-methyl thioglycolate (DMB-S-MTG) as the thioester substrates"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"commentType":"CATALYTIC ACTIVITY","reaction":{"name":"6-carboxyhexanoyl-[ACP] methyl ester + H2O = 6-carboxyhexanoyl-[ACP] + methanol + H(+)","reactionCrossReferences":[{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA:42700"},{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA-COMP:9955"},{"database":"Rhea","id":"RHEA-COMP:10186"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15377"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:15378"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:17790"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:78846"},{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:82735"}],"ecNumber":"3.1.1.85","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11904168"}]}},{"texts":[{"value":"Strongly inhibited by phenylmethylsulfonyl fluoride (PMSF)"}],"commentType":"ACTIVITY REGULATION"},{"commentType":"BIOPHYSICOCHEMICAL PROPERTIES","kineticParameters":{"michaelisConstants":[{"constant":229.0,"unit":"uM","substrate":"DMB-S-MMP (at Ph 7.9 and at room temperature)","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"12732651"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"17625941"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19307763"}]},{"constant":0.29,"unit":"mM","substrate":"pNP-acetate","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"12732651"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"17625941"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19307763"}]},{"constant":0.35,"unit":"mM","substrate":"pNP-propionate","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"12732651"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"17625941"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19307763"}]},{"constant":0.33,"unit":"mM","substrate":"pNP-butyrate","evidences":[{"evidenceCode"